          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"id\":471,\"filmId\":401,\"screeningDate\":\"2026-07-04T18:30\",\"doorsTime\":\"6:00pm\",\"filmTime\":\"6:30pm\",\"capacity\":37,\"ticketsSold\":11,\"price\":1100,\"format\":\"Digital\",\"excludeFromGiftVouchers\":false,\"isPrivate\":false,\"memberOnly\":false,\"film\":{\"id\":401,\"title\":\"WINTER KILLS\",\"description\":\"Nearly two decades after the assassination of President Timothy Kegan, his younger half-brother Nick is drawn back into the case by a dying man who claims to have been one of the killers. What follows is a spiralling investigation through political fixers, organised crime figures, intelligence networks, corporate power, and family mythology. Each apparent revelation only deepens the confusion, turning the search for historical truth into a grotesque tour of American paranoia.\\n\\nAdapted from Richard Condon’s novel, Winter Kills transforms the Kennedy assassination conspiracy cycle into a blackly comic political thriller. William Richert’s film combines post-Watergate distrust with absurdist satire, treating conspiracy less as a solvable mystery than as a permanent condition of power. Its famously troubled production — involving financial collapse, disrupted shooting, and later scandals around its backers — adds to its reputation as one of the strangest American films of the late 1970s.\",\"runtime\":97,\"year\":1979,\"country\":\"USA\",\"director\":\"William Richert\",\"imageUrl\":\"https://60minuteswith.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2020/01/HeaderWinterKillsReview.jpg\",\"imageCropX\":50,\"imageCropY\":50,\"ageCertificate\":\"18\"}}",
